15
00:01:21.623 --> 00:01:23.583
Time to get dressed for school.

16
00:01:24.585 --> 00:01:27.170
His biggest gift to me
was his imagination.

17
00:01:28.297 --> 00:01:30.591
He was a writer. actor. and director.

18
00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:32.914
What. are you kidding me?
The President's gonna be there.

19
00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.554
But he never
quite made it as a filmmaker.

20
00:01:36.722 --> 00:01:37.764
Until one day...

21
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.601
he did.

22
00:01:40.684 --> 00:01:42.144
Action.

23
00:01:44.563 --> 00:01:47.107
He filmed every second of my life.

24
00:01:49.318 --> 00:01:52.237
There was nothing
we wouldn't do for each other.

25
00:01:52.321 --> 00:01:56.408
Can I. um. videotape. um. the world?

26
00:01:57.618 --> 00:01:59.369
That's what
you're doing right now.

27
00:02:00.996 --> 00:02:03.206
You're videotaping the world.

28
00:02:04.708 --> 00:02:06.918
Oh. you're gonna like this.

29
00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:13.925
I'm now that LA cliché

30
00:02:14.009 --> 00:02:17.846
of a struggling actress.
babysitter. wannabe filmmaker.

31
00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:24.853
I work a bunch of jobs.
and I have 50 bucks in the bank.

32
00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:34.738
And then. my dad and I just. uh.
got some really bad news.

33
00:02:39.201 --> 00:02:40.827
It shattered our worlds.

34
00:02:45.749 --> 00:02:46.893
-Bobby. you good?
-Yeah.

35
00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:47.918
Okay. cool.

36
00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.045
This is Daniel's interview.

37
00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.963
I'm terrified.

38
00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:59.012
-Is it a little weird for you?
-Yeah.

39
00:02:59.096 --> 00:03:01.515
But... this is my story.

40
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.895
So. this all started because
you never knew your dad.

41
00:03:06.978 --> 00:03:08.814
Yeah. yeah.

42
00:03:10.774 --> 00:03:15.570
Growing up. was always
skeptical of my father.

43
00:03:15.654 --> 00:03:18.990
Uh. he was Hispanic. of course.
with the last name Arguelles.

44
00:03:19.074 --> 00:03:22.035
And I would go up to him
and put my arm up against his

45
00:03:22.119 --> 00:03:24.319
and I'd say. "Hey. how come
I'm white and you're brown?"

46
00:03:25.372 --> 00:03:29.918
So. when I was 21. I did press my mother

47
00:03:30.502 --> 00:03:32.337
if Jim was my real father.

48
00:03:32.754 --> 00:03:34.297
What did she say?

49
00:03:34.381 --> 00:03:37.050
She confessed. "No. he wasn't."

50
00:03:37.134 --> 00:03:40.303
And I said. "Okay. who's my dad?"

51
00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.389
She said. "I really don't know."

52
00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:48.562
And when I was. you know. 18. 19 years old
in Hollywood. everybody was telling me.

53
00:03:48.645 --> 00:03:51.022
"Wow. you look just like a young Elvis."

54
00:03:51.106 --> 00:03:55.110
I like to sing. I like to dance.
I like to act.

55
00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:56.838
How are you doing. everybody?

56
00:03:56.862 --> 00:03:59.239
That's right. it's me. Elvis Presley.

57
00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:01.867
You know. I'm doing my new album.
and it's called Loving You.

58
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.911
You had a lot of Elvis impressions.
impersonations around the house.

59
00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:06.931
Yeah. I walk around
like I'm Elvis. you know?

60
00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:07.998
I gotta be Elvis. right?

61
00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:12.461
So. you joined Ancestry
to find your father.

62
00:04:12.544 --> 00:04:15.756
It wasn't until seven years later
you found a half-brother.

63
00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:17.549
Right.

64
00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:21.887
He just said.
"Hey. uh. I'm Mike. and we matched."

65
00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.347
And I was like. "Oh. brothers. wow.

66
00:04:24.431 --> 00:04:26.850
Okay. then you know
who your dad is. maybe?"

67
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.477
"Yeah. I know who he is."

68
00:04:33.023 --> 00:04:34.191
You know.

69
00:04:35.776 --> 00:04:36.985
Charles Manson.

70
00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:06.640
♪ My world is a sad world ♪

71
00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:11.853
♪ Often wonder if there's blame ♪

72
00:05:12.813 --> 00:05:14.439
♪ Such a fool ♪

73
00:05:14.523 --> 00:05:17.526
-♪ In a mad. mad world ♪
-Hi. Puppa.

74
00:05:19.486 --> 00:05:21.321
Did you have a good day?

75
00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:23.573
♪ Such a fool in a... ♪

76
00:05:23.657 --> 00:05:27.369
Charles Manson. to me.
he was just this cult leader

77
00:05:27.452 --> 00:05:30.914
and this murderer I had
read about on Wikipedia.

78
00:05:31.832 --> 00:05:33.583
When I found out
he was my grandfather.

79
00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:35.669
I didn't know what he looked like...

80
00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.257
so I decided to YouTube "Charles Manson."

81
00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:43.653
Breaking news. Charles Manson.

82
00:05:43.677 --> 00:05:45.971
cult leader
who led a string of murders

83
00:05:46.054 --> 00:05:48.223
in the 1960s is dead.

84
00:05:48.306 --> 00:05:49.951
With a swastika on his forehead

85
00:05:49.975 --> 00:05:51.601
and eyes wild with violence.

86
00:05:51.685 --> 00:05:54.145
Charles Manson was the face of evil.

87
00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:57.107
Maybe it was that look
in Charles Manson's eyes

88
00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.609
that convinced people
to murder in his name.

89
00:05:59.693 --> 00:06:03.530
He was once called
"the most dangerous man in America."

90
00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:06.825
Forty years later
and the fascination hasn't faded.

91
00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:09.202
People are still interested
in Charles Manson.

92
00:06:09.286 --> 00:06:11.788
Twenty years ago today.
America was stunned

93
00:06:11.872 --> 00:06:14.666
by one of the most brutal
killing sprees in history.

94
00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:16.686
He's been called.
"Madman. mobster.

95
00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.086
and monster. and murderer."

96
00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:21.965
The center of a kind of
horrible fascination that will not die.

97
00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.551
He's a dangerous influence on society.

98
00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:26.803
Dangerous. highly dangerous.

99
00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.158
In a scene
described by one investigator

100
00:06:31.182 --> 00:06:33.435
as reminiscent of a weird religious rite.

101
00:06:33.518 --> 00:06:36.771
five persons. including actress
Sharon Tate. were found dead

102
00:06:36.855 --> 00:06:38.495
at the home of Miss Tate and her husband.

103
00:06:38.565 --> 00:06:40.442
screen director Roman Polanski.

104
00:06:40.525 --> 00:06:42.277
Miss Tate was eight months pregnant

105
00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.239
and was found with a rope around her neck
attached to the body of a man.

106
00:06:46.323 --> 00:06:48.742
Among the other victims
were Hollywood hairstylist

107
00:06:48.825 --> 00:06:51.995
Jay Sebring.
and coffee heiress Abigail Folger.

108
00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:55.916
Cause of death. multiple stab wounds

109
00:06:55.999 --> 00:06:58.084
of the chest and back.

110
00:06:58.168 --> 00:07:00.837
One officer summed up
the murders when he said.

111
00:07:00.921 --> 00:07:05.300
"In all my years. I have never seen
anything like this before."

112
00:07:05.383 --> 00:07:07.263
A short time later. not far away.

113
00:07:07.344 --> 00:07:09.638
a middle-aged couple was found murdered

114
00:07:09.721 --> 00:07:11.306
in similar circumstances.

115
00:07:12.015 --> 00:07:14.142
Leno LaBianca. a supermarket owner.

116
00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:18.688
and his wife had both been
stabbed to death. repeated stab wounds.

117
00:07:18.772 --> 00:07:22.692
On his body. the word "war"
had been carved in the chest.

118
00:07:22.776 --> 00:07:25.195
Then. with blood. the killer had scrawled

119
00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:27.656
the words. "Death to Pigs."

120
00:07:29.491 --> 00:07:31.785
Five members
of a so-called religious cult.

121
00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:36.039
including Charles Manson.
the guru. or high priest.

122
00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:39.459
have been indicted in the murder
of Sharon Tate and six others.

123
00:07:39.542 --> 00:07:42.063
These defendants
literally entered the homes of citizens

124
00:07:42.087 --> 00:07:44.089
of this community at random

125
00:07:44.172 --> 00:07:46.758
and literally slaughtered the victims
in an orgy of murder.

126
00:07:47.258 --> 00:07:48.402
All the elements are present

127
00:07:48.426 --> 00:07:52.180
for one of the most sensational
murder trials in American history.

128
00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:54.182
The involvement of a mystical hippie clan.

129
00:07:54.265 --> 00:07:57.227
which despised
the straight. affluent society.

130
00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.939
Young girls. supposedly under the spell
of a bearded Svengali.

131
00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:03.274
who allegedly masterminded
the seven murders.

132
00:08:03.358 --> 00:08:05.944
In a conspiracy.
the acts of one member

133
00:08:06.027 --> 00:08:07.904
are attributed to all members.

134
00:08:07.988 --> 00:08:11.074
Under that approach. they needn't prove
that he actually did the killing.

135
00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:12.951
It's all a play. isn't it?

136
00:08:13.034 --> 00:08:14.637
The act of the murders themselves

137
00:08:14.661 --> 00:08:17.455
will be the case against the other members
of the so-called Family.

138
00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:21.209
Susan Atkins. Patricia Krenwinkel.

139
00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:24.838
Leslie Van Houten. Charles Watson.

140
00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:26.756
Manson's principle motive

141
00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.842
for ordering the seven
Tate-LaBianca murders

142
00:08:28.925 --> 00:08:31.386
was his fanatical obsession
with "Helter Skelter."

143
00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:35.140
a term he borrowed from the English
musical recording group The Beatles.

144
00:08:35.223 --> 00:08:38.643
To ignite Helter Skelter.
to start the Black/white. uh. revolution.

145
00:08:38.727 --> 00:08:41.229
Manson envisioned that white people

146
00:08:41.312 --> 00:08:43.082
would turn against the Black man
if they thought

147
00:08:43.106 --> 00:08:45.025
the Black man had committed
these seven murders.

148
00:08:45.108 --> 00:08:47.027
The victims. by necessity. had to be white

149
00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.487
and preferably in the white establishment.

150
00:08:50.447 --> 00:08:52.091
The jury hearing the charges

151
00:08:52.115 --> 00:08:54.409
against Charles Manson
and three girl members

152
00:08:54.492 --> 00:08:57.287
of his so-called Family
brought in its verdict.

153
00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:01.291
All were found guilty
of murder in the first degree.

154
00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:03.585
Manson then shouted at the jurors.

155
00:09:03.668 --> 00:09:04.919
"You're all guilty.

156
00:09:05.003 --> 00:09:07.797
We are still not allowed
to put on a defense."

157
00:09:07.881 --> 00:09:10.800
The press has been the mouthpiece
of the prosecution.

158
00:09:10.884 --> 00:09:14.095
And they've succeeded in brainwashing
a lot of people.

159
00:09:14.179 --> 00:09:16.806
Charlie never preached a "Race War."

160
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.309
-That's what the DA gave you for reality.
-Okay.

161
00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:20.792
He stood in the courtroom and said.

162
00:09:20.852 --> 00:09:22.532
"This man did this.
and this man did that."

163
00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:23.563
and you all believed him.

164
00:09:31.613 --> 00:09:33.823
Those first few days
after I found out...

165
00:09:35.533 --> 00:09:38.787
I don't think I've ever been
that low in my life.

166
00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:40.997
I kinda hated myself.

167
00:09:43.249 --> 00:09:44.667
I hated him.

168
00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:53.593
I've always been the good one.

169
00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:56.971
I've always been the one. or the kid.

170
00:09:57.055 --> 00:09:59.891
with her head screwed on right.

171
00:09:59.974 --> 00:10:03.353
I would always get these. like.
integrity or caring awards. and...

172
00:10:04.896 --> 00:10:08.691
all of the teachers would tell my parents
how nice I was to everybody and...

173
00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:12.529
if someone was lonely.
I'd go sit next to them.

174
00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:16.407
or if I saw someone bullied.
I'd stand up against the bully and...

175
00:10:18.159 --> 00:10:22.831
I always took really great pride
in being this moral. good person.

176
00:10:25.208 --> 00:10:27.377
And then you find out
your grandfather's Manson...

177
00:10:32.215 --> 00:10:33.967
The most hated man.

178
00:10:35.802 --> 00:10:39.681
How could my blood do something so...

179
00:10:40.473 --> 00:10:41.474
atrocious?

180
00:10:41.558 --> 00:10:43.393
How could my blood...

181
00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.646
be the cause of other people's bloodshed?

182
00:10:48.648 --> 00:10:50.525
If it's true that there
was something in him

183
00:10:50.608 --> 00:10:53.903
that made him this satanic cult leader...

184
00:10:55.446 --> 00:10:57.866
do my dad and I
have that in our blood too?

185
00:10:59.617 --> 00:11:00.743
I have his eyes.

186
00:11:02.036 --> 00:11:03.872
His hands are my dad's.

187
00:11:05.415 --> 00:11:06.833
It fucks with you.

188
00:11:10.128 --> 00:11:13.548
Was he born evil or was it something else?

189
00:11:14.424 --> 00:11:16.426
I need to find out what made him.

190
00:11:20.722 --> 00:11:24.726
I don't know. I just feel like
I. for sure. need a therapist.

191
00:11:28.938 --> 00:11:31.274
I'm the spawn of the spawn
of the spawn of Satan.

192
00:11:31.357 --> 00:11:33.693
You know. he was called second to Hitler.

193
00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.158
Um. I don't want
to minimize what you're sharing...

194
00:11:41.075 --> 00:11:44.370
but I may challenge
parts of your narrative.

195
00:11:45.455 --> 00:11:50.585
Being the descendant
of somebody labeled "a monster among men."

196
00:11:50.668 --> 00:11:54.380
it is your God-given right
to break that down

197
00:11:54.464 --> 00:11:56.549
and to make sense of that.

198
00:11:56.633 --> 00:12:00.470
I think a part of the information
that you're coming into

199
00:12:00.553 --> 00:12:03.973
is that there are parts
of Charlie's narrative

200
00:12:04.057 --> 00:12:05.558
that was sensationalized.

201
00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:09.812
If you're not careful
as one of his descendants.

202
00:12:09.896 --> 00:12:12.357
you will carry the sensationalization.

203
00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.651
-and that is not yours to carry.
-Yeah.

204
00:12:14.734 --> 00:12:18.071
So. breaking down
the sensationalization of him.

205
00:12:18.154 --> 00:12:19.155
what is true?

206
00:12:19.239 --> 00:12:21.199
What is accurate? What is real?

207
00:12:21.282 --> 00:12:23.243
So I am clear about what I need to break

208
00:12:23.326 --> 00:12:25.119
-and what I don't need to carry.
-Yeah.

209
00:12:25.203 --> 00:12:28.831
Why would I carry. um. fabricated wounds?

210
00:12:28.915 --> 00:12:30.124
-Right?
-Okay.

211
00:12:30.208 --> 00:12:31.626
-Yeah?
-Yeah.

212
00:12:34.128 --> 00:12:37.882
I'm gonna face him.
I'm gonna face what he did.

213
00:12:37.966 --> 00:12:39.550
I am not a journalist.

214
00:12:39.634 --> 00:12:42.345
It's going to be really hard
to dig out the truth.

215
00:12:42.428 --> 00:12:44.305
and I have to try and stay objective.

216
00:12:46.557 --> 00:12:48.643
To understand his true pathology.

217
00:12:48.726 --> 00:12:50.144
my gut tells me I should track down

218
00:12:50.228 --> 00:12:53.273
the only surviving prosecutor.
Stephen Kay.

219
00:12:53.356 --> 00:12:55.817
I am just giving you a call.

220
00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.861
I am directing a documentary
about Charles Manson.

221
00:13:01.406 --> 00:13:04.325
Okay. um... Hmm.

222
00:13:04.409 --> 00:13:07.620
I've reached out to victims' relatives.

223
00:13:07.704 --> 00:13:13.001
It's actually kind of a different approach
than you might think.

224
00:13:13.084 --> 00:13:16.462
But people seem to not want to talk to me

225
00:13:16.546 --> 00:13:19.716
because I'm
Charles Manson's granddaughter.

226
00:13:19.799 --> 00:13:21.009
I see.

227
00:13:22.218 --> 00:13:23.594
Okay. thank you.

228
00:13:25.263 --> 00:13:27.223
Now that I'm Manson's granddaughter.

229
00:13:28.141 --> 00:13:31.561
people assume that I'm not so moral.

230
00:13:34.731 --> 00:13:36.607
But I'm the same person as I was

231
00:13:36.691 --> 00:13:38.985
before I found out
I'm Manson's granddaughter.

232
00:13:40.194 --> 00:13:41.946
I have not changed.

233
00:13:44.657 --> 00:13:47.493
I'm not really sure
what I'm getting myself into.

234
00:13:47.577 --> 00:13:49.329
but I'll take anybody.

235
00:13:53.875 --> 00:13:56.294
I tracked down a friend
of my grandfather's.

236
00:13:57.045 --> 00:13:58.921
Charlie called him "Black Wolf."

237
00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:04.302
He's an ex-con.
someone who's lived in the underworld.

238
00:14:05.511 --> 00:14:07.722
He prefers to keep his face off camera.

239
00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:10.892
and I'm nervous to meet someone

240
00:14:10.975 --> 00:14:13.019
who's come face-to-face with Manson.

241
00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:19.256
You made a promise to stay in the shadows
for my grandfather.

242
00:14:20.818 --> 00:14:22.862
So. what made you
make this exception for me?

243
00:14:23.696 --> 00:14:26.324
I think
'cause you're his blood. for one thing.

244
00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:29.994
And I think that your intentions are pure.

245
00:14:31.287 --> 00:14:32.830
Uh. seemingly to me.

246
00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.917
What was
your relationship with him?

247
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:40.671
I wrote Charlie in 2007. just a letter.

